 ],
 "literal": "In the deity-residing banyan tree's broad branches, many fruits / 'Yesterday we ate,' without thinking, again / going does not cease, the bustling flock of birds. / Such are they, may they live long, the suppliants! Them / welcoming with patronage, the men of great deeds, / their wealth becomes their wealth; / their poverty becomes their poverty.",
 "translation": "Like the bustling flock of birds that never cease to go to the deity-residing banyan tree with broad branches and abundant fruit, never thinking, 'We ate there only yesterday,' so are the suppliants—may they live long! The wealth of the great men who welcome and patronize them is truly the suppliants' own wealth, and the poverty of those patrons is the suppliants' own poverty.",
 "modern": "தெய்வம் உறையும் ஆலமரத்தின் பரந்த கிளைகளில் உள்ள பல பழங்களை, 'நேற்றுதான் உண்டோம்' என்று நினைக்காமல் பறவைக் கூட்டங்கள் மீண்டும் மீண்டும் தேடிச் செல்லும். இரவலர்களும் அத்தகையவர்களே; அவர்கள் வாழ்க! அவர்களை வரவேற்று ஆதரிக்கும் வள்ளல்களின் செல்வமே இரவலர்களின் செல்வமாகும்; அவ்வள்ளல்களின் வறுமையே இரவலர்களின் வறுமையுமாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Puram genre, under the theme of 'Parisil Kada Nilai' (demanding gifts). The poet, Perumpatumanar, compares suppliants to a flock of birds that constantly visit a fruitful banyan tree. Just as the birds do not hesitate to return to the tree day after day, forgetting that they ate there yesterday, the suppliants continuously seek out generous patrons. The poet praises these patrons, stating that their wealth is effectively the wealth of the suppliants, and their poverty is also shared by the suppliants. The key words are 'iravalar' (suppliants), 'puḷḷiṉam' (flock of birds), and 'uṭaimai' (wealth).",
